= Read PVD 1.0 =

#type: node
#context: obj
#internal: readPVD::1.0
#tags: fem, results, polyfem, vtk, paraview

"""Loads PolyFEM results (a .pvd collection of VTU or VTK-HDF frames) and shows them: deformation, colored fields, glyphs, fibers, clipping, probing and legends."""

== Overview ==

Point __PVD File__ at the `.pvd` file PolyFEM wrote in its output folder
(`output/sim.pvd` by default). A run written by the PolyFEM node plays on
that node's timeline (Houdini time is simulation time, see Timeline below);
any other run shows one saved time step per frame. The PolyFEM node's
__Open Results__ button creates and sets up this node for you.

On loading a file the node picks the field to color by (von Mises stress,
exported or derived from the deformation gradient and stress; otherwise the
displacement), sets the color range from the frame on screen and the last
frame, and frames the viewport on the result. Choosing another field or
value re-ranges the colors the same way, unless __Lock Displayed Range__ is
on.

Every parameter has a tooltip: hover over its label for details.

== Quick start ==

# Set __PVD File__ (or press __Open Results__ on the PolyFEM node).
# Scrub the playbar. __Refresh / Set Playbar__ picks up the steps of a run
  that is still going.
# __Display tab__: choose the __Color Field__ and __Field Value To Display__
  (a component, magnitude, principal value ...). __Auto Range: All Frames__
  finds the range over the whole run.
# __Smooth Field Across Elements__ averages element values onto the nodes;
  where bodies touch (with body ids exported) each body keeps its own
  values.
# __Visible Bodies__ shows or hides bodies, __Clip / Slice__ cuts into the
  model, __Probe__ reads values at a clicked point, __Legend__ adds a color
  bar.

== Timeline ==

__Time Mapping__ says which time step a frame shows:

* __Automatic__ (the default): the PolyFEM node's own timeline when the run
  was written by it (its __Start Frame__ and __Time Scale__, recorded in
  `input/hda_scene.json`), so a keyed load or a moving obstacle and its
  result are on the same frame; one frame per step otherwise.
* __Simulation Time__: your own __Start Frame__ and __Time Scale__.
* __One Frame per Output Step__: frame k shows step k.

On a timeline a frame shows the last step at or before its simulation time.
__Simulation Time__ (the read-only line under it) shows the step on screen
and its time.

== Force curves ==

__Analysis > Force Curves__ measures forces on the sidesets and obstacles
of a run written by the PolyFEM node with __Nodal Forces__ on (new nodes
start with it on). For each set, per time step:

* __Reaction__: the force a fixed or moved sideset (or an obstacle's
  prescribed motion) applies to the body -- minus the sum of every nodal
  force PolyFEM writes there. Zero where nothing is prescribed.
* __Contact Force__: contact plus friction forces on those nodes; on an
  obstacle, the force the body applies to it.
* The mean displacement of the set.

__Compute Force Curves__ reads every step and writes `force_curves.csv`
next to the PVD file; __At This Frame__ shows the values on screen; __Show
Chart__ draws one curve (force against displacement or time) with a marker
that follows the playbar. Nodes on an edge between two sidesets count in
both. Adhesion and Rayleigh-damping forces are not written by PolyFEM and
are not included.

== HDF5 results ==

PolyFEM's default output is compressed VTK-HDF. This node bundles the h5py
reader for Houdini 22 on macOS (Apple silicon and Intel), Linux x86_64
(glibc 2.28 or newer) and Windows x64, and extracts it on the first HDF5
load. On any other system, install h5py into Houdini's Python; the PolyFEM
node writes XML `.vtu` files instead when it sees that this node could not
read HDF5.

== Troubleshooting ==

table>>
    tr>>
        th>> What you see
        th>> What to do
    tr>>
        td>> One flat color
        td>> All values are equal in the frames used for the range (often
             frame 0, before anything moved): press __Auto Range: All
             Frames__.
    tr>>
        td>> The neutral Unavailable Field Color
        td>> The chosen field is not in this frame: pick another, or set
             __Field Availability Scope__ to list fields of every frame.
    tr>>
        td>> "Mesh topology changes over time"
        td>> The run remeshed: turn on __Remeshing Mode__.
    tr>>
        td>> "This VTK-HDF result needs h5py"
        td>> See HDF5 results above.
    tr>>
        td>> Memory grows while scrubbing
        td>> Lower __Cache Memory Limit__, or turn off __Cache Frames__.

